# Browser control: the real Chrome, never a fresh profile

Owner preference (2026-09-01, extended 2026-09-03). The user's real Chrome
carries what an automation browser cannot reproduce: 18 authenticated
profiles, the 1Password extension in each, and the trusted-device state banks
demand. A browser launched with a temporary or isolated profile has none of it
and is unusable for any logged-in site, so do not launch one for that.

1. **chrome-cli first** (`/opt/homebrew/bin/chrome-cli`) for anything it
   answers: list, open, close and activate tabs and windows across every
   profile, navigate, reload, `source`, `execute <js>`. One Bash call, no MCP
   session. Wrap `execute` in `timeout 20`.
2. **Attach to the running Chrome for the rest**: DOM snapshots and element
   interaction, screenshots, network, console, traces, dialogs. Remote
   debugging is user-enabled in `chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging`
   (Chrome 144+, on since 2026-09-02), so chrome-devtools MCP runs with
   `--autoConnect` and Playwright MCP or CLI connect with `--extension`
   (Playwright MCP Bridge, installed per profile) or `--browser chrome`.
   Chrome shows a permission dialog on connect; that is the user's click, not
   a failure. Claude in Chrome only acts inside its own tab group and needs a
   manual Connect per profile, so it is the last of these, not the first.
3. **A separate browser only when unavoidable** (parallel runs, tests of our
   own app that must not touch real sessions), and then always a persistent
   per-identity profile under `~/.agent-chrome/<identity>` on the real Google
   Chrome binary, with the 1Password extension installed there once. Never
   `--isolated`, a tmp user-data-dir, or bundled Chromium for a logged-in
   site. CDP on the default user-data-dir needs the toggle above; on any other
   directory it needs `--user-data-dir` (Chrome 136+).
4. Orca's embedded browser is driven through the `orca-cli` skill, and desktop
   app windows or webviews outside Chrome through `computer-use`.
